        "q": "How would you design an alarm clock app for visually impaired users?",
    "tracks": ["B2C / Consumer PM","Hardware PM"],
        "subcategory": "product_design",
        "a": "Recommendation: Design for completely blind users first-sharpest constraints, largest underserved need-then layer low-vision support post-validation.\n\nData Validation First\nBefore building anything, I'd interview 10 blind users to confirm the core hypothesis: they currently rely on sighted assistance to set alarms. I'd also audit VoiceOver/TalkBack telemetry to identify error rates and interaction bottlenecks. If the hypothesis fails, pivot immediately.\n\nV1 Core Features\n- Voice setup: \"Set alarm for 7am weekdays\" with immediate audio confirmation-eliminates visual dependency entirely\n- Distinct haptic patterns: Unique rhythms per alarm so users identify which triggered without needing audio\n- Native screen-reader parity: VoiceOver/TalkBack support built in from day one, never retrofitted\n\nPilot Design\nTwo-week unmoderated usability test, 15 blind participants: Group A uses voice-only setup; Group B uses gesture-based touch navigation. Primary gate: completing alarm setup without sighted assistance.\n\nSuccess Metrics\n- Independent setup completion rate (target ≥75%)\n- Time-to-first-alarm vs. sighted user benchmark\n- False alarm rate (reliability signal)\n- Day-7 and Day-30 retention\n\nTrade-offs\n- Voice adds engineering complexity but eliminates the highest-friction point-worth prioritizing over touch-only V1\n- Morning briefing features (weather, calendar) sound valuable but dilute reliability focus; defer to V2 once core is proven\n- Haptic-only mode risks missing users who need audio reinforcement; ship both, measure preference split\n\nDo not ship until 10 users complete setup independently in testing.",
