        "q": "Walk me through how you would prioritize a backlog of 20 feature requests.",
    "tracks": ["B2B / Enterprise PM","Product Operations","Internal Tools PM"],
        "subcategory": "product_strategy",
        "a": "Framework: Score → Validate → Pilot → Decide\n\nFor a growth-stage SaaS with 3–5 shippable slots per quarter, I'd run this process:\n\nStep 1: RICE Scoring with source weighting.\nTag each request by origin-churn interview, support volume, sales blocker, or executive intuition. A feature surfacing in 40% of churn interviews outweighs one from a single enterprise request. Apply RICE, but discount Confidence for gut-feel estimates (0.5x multiplier) versus data-backed ones (1.0x). This collapses 20 items to a shortlist of 5.\n\nStep 2: Validate assumptions before committing.\nSpend one week pressure-testing the top 5-pull support ticket frequency, check retention impact of analogous features in comparable products, run 5 targeted user calls. This surfaces whether we're solving a real problem or a loud one.\n\nStep 3: A/B test the top candidate.\nRather than full-build, ship an MVP to 20% of users for 30 days. Success thresholds: feature adoption above 25%, measurable lift in the North Star metric (e.g., weekly active usage), and 15%+ reduction in related support tickets. No movement means we re-rank before over-investing.\n\nTrade-offs I'd flag:\n- RICE rewards breadth over intensity-a feature 10% of users need daily can outperform one 50% use monthly\n- Validation adds one week upfront but prevents wasting an entire quarter on the wrong bet\n- Frameworks organize judgment; they don't replace it\n\nThe output isn't a ranked list-it's a shared, testable hypothesis the team owns.",
