        "q": "What metrics would you track for a new social media app at launch?",
    "tracks": ["B2C / Consumer PM","Growth PM","Data / AI PM"],
        "subcategory": "product_strategy",
        "a": "Framework: Track metrics across three horizons-activation, retention, and content health-anchored to one North Star.\n\nNorth Star: Daily Active Creators, not DAUs. Social apps die from content supply failure, not demand failure.\n\nData validation first: Instrument the onboarding funnel on day one. Run a 2-week cohort analysis on beta users segmented by acquisition channel. If D7 retention varies >30% across channels, reallocate budget before scaling spend.\n\nLaunch metrics (90-day window):\n- Activation: Onboarding completion rate; time-to-first-post (target <5 min); % posting in session one (target >15%)\n- Retention: D1/D7/D30 retention by cohort and channel; returning creator rate (posted more than once?)\n- Content health: Posts-per-DAU (flag below 0.5 as lurker-only problem); creator-to-consumer ratio (baseline target 1:9)\n\nPilot: A/B test two onboarding flows-post-first versus follow-first. Hypothesis: post-first increases D7 creator retention by 20%. Run 3 weeks, 50/50 split, 5,000 users per arm minimum.\n\nTrade-offs I'm managing:\n- Optimizing creator rate risks pressuring new users before they have context\n- Aggressive content surfacing helps creators but may degrade early consumer experience and suppress return visits\n\nIgnore at launch: downloads, revenue, viral coefficient-all premature before product-market fit is confirmed.\n\nWeekly qualitative check: \"Would you post here again?\" catches signal before retention curves have enough data to be meaningful.",
