        "q": "How do you define a good product?",
    "tracks": ["B2C / Consumer PM","B2B / Enterprise PM","Product Marketing Manager"],
        "a": "A good product sits at the intersection of three things: real user value, frictionless delivery, and sustainable economics.\n\nThe three-part definition:\n\n1. Solves a real problem - Users describe it as \"finally fixed,\" not \"nice to have.\" Validated through discovery interviews where you hear unprompted frustration, not polite interest.\n\n2. Delivers frictionlessly - Users reach their goal without support tickets or tutorials. Measured by task completion rate and time-to-first-value.\n\n3. Works economically - LTV exceeds CAC with unit economics that improve as the product matures, not just at scale.\n\nData validation before declaring success: I'd examine retention curve shape (does it flatten, indicating habit formation?), NPS paired with open-ended follow-ups, and organic referral rate. Word-of-mouth is the strongest signal - users staking their reputation on a recommendation.\n\nSpecific pilot: Run an A/B test on two onboarding flows - one optimized for feature breadth, one for time-to-first-value. Primary metrics: Day-7 retention and referral rate within 30 days. Secondary: support ticket volume. The winning variant reveals whether core value is strong enough to drive itself.\n\nCritical trade-off: Engagement metrics can masquerade as genuine value. High DAUs built on anxiety loops or dark patterns satisfy economic metrics temporarily but collapse retention once users recognize manipulation - and regulators increasingly penalize it.\n\nA good product grows because users recommend it unprompted. Everything else is a lagging indicator of whether you got that right.",
