        "q": "Design a product for college students to manage their academic workload.",
    "tracks": ["B2C / Consumer PM","Growth PM"],
        "subcategory": "product_design",
        "a": "Recommendation: Build a syllabus-to-schedule engine for first-year college students, solving deadline blindness before it becomes academic failure.\n\nWhy this segment: First-years carry the highest dropout risk, weakest organizational systems, and strongest openness to new tools. This maximizes both impact and adoption probability.\n\nData validation first: Before writing code, interview 15 first-year students across 3 universities representing different institution types. Test three hypotheses: (1) calendar usage is inconsistent, (2) manual entry is the primary abandonment point, (3) workload clustering around midterms drives the sharpest anxiety spikes. If fewer than 70% identify deadline blindness as a top-three stressor, reframe the problem before proceeding.\n\nCore V1 features:\n- Syllabus OCR: Upload PDF → auto-extract all deadlines, eliminating the single biggest friction point\n- Workload heatmap: Visual load distribution showing \"14 hours due Friday\" to force proactive rebalancing\n- Daily task prompt: Surfaces 2-3 prioritized tasks based on deadline proximity and estimated effort\n\nPilot: Partner with one university's first-year orientation program. Run a randomized A/B test - 200 students with the app versus 200 without - across one full semester.\n\nSuccess metrics: On-time submission rate (primary), week-6 weekly active usage (early retention signal), and end-of-semester GPA delta (lagging outcome).\n\nKey trade-offs: OCR accuracy is a real reliability risk - ship manual entry in V1, OCR in V2 once parsing is validated. Deliberately exclude AI writing assistance; it dilutes the core value proposition and creates university policy exposure that kills institutional partnerships.\n\nMonetization path: Freemium for one course, institutional licensing at scale.",
