        "a": "Random Forest: ensemble of decision trees trained on random subsets of data and features. Each tree independent, predictions averaged. Works: train 100 trees on bootstrap samples, each tree considers random feature subset, average predictions. Single tree: overfits easily (high variance), fast. Random Forest: bagging reduces variance, robust. Con: slower, less interpretable. Gradient Boosting (XGBoost): sequential trees. Each corrects previous tree's errors. Slower training (sequential), better accuracy typically, harder to tune. Tradeoff: RF simpler, less tuning, parallelizes. XGBoost more accurate, requires tuning (learning rate, depth), sequential (slower). When pick RF: want simplicity, no hyperparameter tuning, fast training. When pick XGBoost: accuracy critical, willing to tune, have time. Real: competition? Use XGBoost (accuracy paramount). Production system with limited training time? RF. Interpretability matters? Both have tree-based explanations, but single decision tree most interpretable. Speed critical? RF parallelizes, XGBoost sequential. Modern: LightGBM (gradient boosting, faster than XGBoost). Most teams use XGBoost or LightGBM now, RF less common."
