        "a": "Traditional ML: hand-crafted features+simple models (trees, regression). Deep learning: learns features automatically from raw data via neural networks. DL pros: handles unstructured data (images, text), finds complex patterns. Cons: needs massive data (100K+ samples), slow training, black-box (hard to interpret). When overkill: structured tabular data with <1M rows, features already engineered, interpretability needed, limited budget. Example: predicting customer churn from behavioral features (login frequency, support tickets, purchases). Traditional ML (gradient boosting) works great, 95% accuracy, fast, interpretable (feature importance). DL unnecessary. When necessary: unstructured data (images, audio, text), need state-of-art accuracy, have massive data. Example: object detection in retail images. Traditional CV (hand-crafted features) fails. Deep CNN required. Sentiment analysis of 10M customer reviews-embedding models (transformers) better than bag-of-words. Real decision: churn prediction? Use XGBoost (structured, interpretable). Product image classification? Use ResNet (DL, image data). Text recommendations? Use transformers (DL, text). Most companies: DL on 5% of problems (images/NLP), traditional ML on 95% (structured data). DL is trend, traditional ML is workhorse."
