        "a": "Bayes theorem: P(defective | positive) = P(positive | defective) × P(defective) / P(positive). Given: P(defective) = 0.01, sensitivity = 99%, false positive rate = 5%. Step 1-P(positive | defective) = 0.99. P(positive | not defective) = 0.05. Step 2-P(not defective) = 0.99. Step 3-P(positive) = P(positive | defective) × P(defective) + P(positive | not defective) × P(not defective) = 0.99 × 0.01 + 0.05 × 0.99 = 0.0099 + 0.0495 = 0.0594. Step 4-P(defective | positive) = 0.99 × 0.01 / 0.0594 = 0.0099 / 0.0594 approximately 0.167 (about 16.7%). Counter-intuitive: 99% accurate test, but positive means only 17% chance of defect! Reason: disease very rare (1%). False positives (5% of 99% = 5% of normal items) outnumber true positives (99% of 1% = 1% of defective items). When defect rate increases to 50%, posterior jumps to ~95%. Base rate dominance. Real-world manufacturing: if you use test result alone, you will reject 17 good items for every 1 actual defect (wasteful). Better approach: two-stage testing. Positive items retested, or use test to rank items (test score) rather than binary yes/no. Product quality control requires understanding base rates and balancing false positives (waste) vs false negatives (customer complaints)."
