        "domain": "ecommerce",
        "company": "Flipkart",
        "a": "Omnichannel effect: store visit → product education → higher confidence → online purchase of premium products (not cheaper). Selection bias possible (customers buying premium already likely to visit stores), but likely some causal effect of in-store education. Hypothesis: in-store consultation (optometrist advice, frame try-on) reduces perceived risk, enables higher-value purchase online. Marketing optimization: (1) Segment customers: store visitors vs online-only. Store visitors higher LTV. (2) Attribution-did store visit cause online purchase, or coincidence? Use propensity score matching or controlled experiments. (3) Marketing spend allocation: if causal, invest in store traffic (drive-to-store campaigns). (4) Funnel optimization: online → invite to store → online purchase. Orchestrate journey. Recommended strategy: (1) Drive-to-store campaigns: target high-potential online browsers with store visit incentives (free eye exam, 10% coupon redeemable in-store). (2) Store-to-online: post-visit email campaign (product recommendations based on tried frames). (3) Loyalty program: store visit + online purchase earns points. Stickiness. (4) Inventory sync: online shows \"try in store\" button. Integrates channels. (5) Testing: A/B test. Cohort 1: drive online traffic only. Cohort 2: drive store visits (same budget). Measure 30-day online spend. If cohort 2 > cohort 1 by 40%, strategy validated. Attribution modeling: (1) First-click: credits store visit (first touchpoint). (2) Last-click: credits email/online ad (last before purchase). (3) Multi-touch: credits both (store 50%, email 50%). (4) Time-decay: recent touchpoints more credit. (5) Data-driven: measure incremental impact of store visit on subsequent online purchase using controlled experiments or regression. Real challenge: feedback loop. Store visitors already higher-intent (self-selected). Must isolate causal effect. Experiment: (1) GEO experiment: some cities heavy store marketing, others no. Measure online purchase difference. Controls for base rates. (2) Sequential experiment: drive online traffic via ads. For those who click, randomly nudge 50% to store, 50% to online. Measure online spend of store-nudged 30 days later. Causal effect revealed. (3) Longitudinal: new customers. Track first touchpoint (store vs online). Measure subsequent lifetime value, LTV ratio. Store-first likely 30-50% higher due to product education + trust-building."
